Changes: pure Go random challenge generation, arithmetic/DOM expected-state
calculation, raw-DEFLATE encoding, validation, and browser detector. The
embedded browser template and full list of 18 automated-browser checks were
extracted from the pinned upstream generator at development time; all
requests use Go only. Checks are injected in every mode, including
observation mode (BlockAutomatedBrowsers=false): a client-reported hit then
surfaces as the instr_blocked_reported risk flag instead of a failure, while
blocking mode keeps failing with instr_automated_browser as before.
The generated template preserves browser environment checks and 17-font probe.
Each challenge randomizes its ID, four variables, initial values, arithmetic,
final salts, helper identifiers, and marker hash seed. Check selection samples
CheckCount of the 18 checks: default 18 when blocking automated browsers,
otherwise the upstream default 8; explicit values must be 1..18 (out of range
is rejected, never clamped). Selection is per-challenge random for direct
callers, or deterministically pinned per scope when a trusted ScopeSeed is set
(the embedding Engine derives one from HMAC-SHA256(secret, "cap:instr-sel:"+
scope) so one client cannot re-roll for an easier sample). Only which checks
are injected is deterministic; names, values, arithmetic, and salts stay
per-challenge random. The default obfuscation level is 6 when blocking
automated browsers and 3 otherwise; explicit levels 1..10 always win. All
marker constants are recalculated in Go using FNV1a semantics.
Runtime JS engines, Node, Bun, and JavaScript obfuscator are not required.
All levels 1-10 are supported with explicitly documented Go-native transforms:
1-3: upstream lightweight randomized template family (same upstream behavior).
4: shuffled, deduplicated plaintext string pool.
5: shuffled string pool with UTF-16 Unicode escapes (no runtime decoder).
6: shuffled UTF-8/base64 string pool with eager data-only decoding.
7: random-key byte XOR plus base64 string pool.
8: per-string random-key UTF-16 XOR pool.
9: same per-string encoding split into chunks of at most eight code units.
10: same per-string encoding split into chunks of at most three code units.
The extra encoding and finer chunks increase generated code/data and decoding
work; they are not encryption, a security boundary, or a bot-resistance guarantee.
The transform recognizes this fixed template's string/comment/regex grammar;
it is not a general-purpose JavaScript parser or arbitrary-code minifier.
It intentionally retains whitespace rather than risking automatic semicolon
insertion or regex semantics. Generated source is never passed to eval/Function
by the obfuscator. The original browser environment checks still use eval and
Function exactly as upstream and require appropriate instrumentation CSP.
Upstream optionally uses esbuild and javascript-obfuscator; when unavailable it
falls back to strings. This implementation provides the protocol/behavioral
fallback with Go-native level-specific encodings, NOT the optional package's
RC4, control-flow flattening, dead-code injection, self-defending, debugger or
console-disabling features. Do not claim equal obfuscation or security strength.
Detector fixtures record pinned upstream decisions on JSON round-tripped probes.
The optional Node test checks generated arithmetic only, not browser semantics.
CAP_BROWSER_TEST=1 enables a real Chrome test that executes the full generated
script in an allow-scripts sandbox iframe and validates its state and probe.
A 64 KiB input limit is an intentional hardening difference from upstream.
Detection IDs, gating decisions, and risk flags match the upstream fixtures;
not every human-readable diagnostic detail is copied verbatim.
